#Problem APEX 2.4.36
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation)
## DBsection(Quotient rule (without trigonometric functions))
## KEYWORDS('derivative','rules', 'horizontal tangent line')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(3)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('2.4')
## Problem1('36')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

$a = random(1, 6) * random(-1, 1, 2);
$b = $a * random(2, 7);
$c = random(1, 15) * random(-1, 1, 2);

$k   = $a * $b;
$num = "$k x";
$den = "$b x + $c";

$num1 = $a * $b * $c;

BEGIN_PGML
Let [``  f(x) = \frac{[$num]}{[$den]}  ``].

Find the [`x`]-value(s) where the graph of [`y=f(x)`] has a horizontal tangent line.  
Separate multiple answers with commas.  
Enter *DNE* if there are no such [`x`].

[`x = `][_]{Compute("DNE")}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
It is impossible for [```
\begin{aligned}
f'(x) & =  \frac{[$k] \cdot ([$den]) - ([$k] x) \cdot ([$b])}{([$den])^2} \\
    & =  \frac{[$num1]}{([$den])^2}  
    \end{aligned}
    ```]
to be zero.

Since the derivative is never zero, no line tangent to the graph of [` f `] can ever be horizontal.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
